{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 9, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a powerful nobleman who ruled the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. His life was filled with political intrigue and military conflict as he navigated the complex landscape of the Crusader States. He served as regent for the young King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem and played a crucial role in the kingdom affairs before his death. His lineage traced back to Raymond II who was assassinated by the Assassins in 1152. Raymond III inherited the county at a young age and faced immediate challenges from his relatives who disputed his claim to power.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond III was born around 1140 and became Count of Tripoli after his father died. He spent many years as a prisoner of Nur ad-Din after being captured in battle. This imprisonment lasted for fifteen years and marked a significant period of his life. He was released in 1173 and returned to Tripoli to reclaim his title. His long absence created a power vacuum that others tried to fill during his captivity. He had to fight to restore his authority over the city and the surrounding territories.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nUpon returning to the Holy Land he became a central figure in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He served as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. This role placed him in direct conflict with other nobles like Guy of Lusignan. Raymond III opposed Guy because he believed Guy was unfit to rule the kingdom in the absence of a healthy monarch. He sought alliances with other powerful figures to maintain stability within the realm. His political maneuvering often put him at odds with the Byzantine Empire and the Muslim states.\n\n*Later Years and Death*\nRaymond III died in 1187 just before the disastrous Battle of Hattin. Some historians believe his death removed a key check on the power of Guy of Lusignan. This lack of opposition may have contributed to the poor decisions made during the campaign against Saladin. The County of Tripoli survived the battle but lost many of its territories shortly after. Raymond left behind a legacy of diplomatic skill and military service despite his many setbacks. His story illustrates the fragility of the Crusader States during this turbulent era. The county continued under his son Raymond IV who faced new threats from the rising power of Saladin. The history of this period remains a subject of intense study for scholars of the medieval world.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0014746328}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.6689314e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.013704256}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.045830276}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.007237755}, {"token": " powerful", "logprob": -0.93945587}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.004984922}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.23732711}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.02740398}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.15578414}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.046296198}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.0006933231}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.0039915126}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.027734231}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.004250906}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -1.7881409e-06}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -5.8412725e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.030833835}, {"token": " His", "logprob": -1.5020941}, {"token": " life", "logprob": -0.04720258}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.28207487}, {"token": " filled", "logprob": -0.82292724}, {"token": " with", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " political", "logprob": -0.026848808}, {"token": " intrigue", "logprob": -0.14813146}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.00075124105}, {"token": " military", "logprob": -0.0024074065}, {"token": " conflict", "logprob": -0.08552473}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.117146075}, {"